# From peace to war, 1919–1939
subtitle: "How a resented peace, a weak League and a slump made war possible in Europe, and why Hitler's unanswered gambles made it come in 1939."
thumb: "card charcoal band \"Every gamble paid off. Until one didn't.\" note \"1919 → 1939\""

## From peace to war, 1919–1939
sources:
    - id: reparations
      title: World War I reparations
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2025
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/World_War_I_reparations
      finding: 132 bn gold marks set in the 1921 London Schedule; about 20 bn actually paid, 1920–1931
    - id: taylor
      title: The Origins of the Second World War
      authors: A. J. P. Taylor
      year: 1961
      finding: Hitler as an opportunist exploiting chances the Western powers gave him, rather than following a blueprint
    - id: overy
      title: The Origins of the Second World War
      authors: Richard Overy
      year: 1987
      finding: 'The war grew from an unstable international system: declining British and French empires against ambitious new powers'
    - id: kershaw
      title: 'Hitler, 1936–1945: Nemesis'
      authors: Ian Kershaw
      year: 2000
      finding: Hitler's aims were consistent; each unopposed success emboldened the next gamble
    - id: britannica_versailles
      title: 'Germany: The Treaty of Versailles'
      authors: Encyclopaedia Britannica
      year: 2024
      url: https://www.britannica.com/place/Germany/The-Treaty-of-Versailles
      finding: Germany lost about 13% of its territory and 10% of its population
    - id: nazi_votes
      title: Nazi Party election results
      authors: Wikipedia
      year: 2025
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nazi_Party_election_results
      finding: 2.6% in May 1928; 37.3% in July 1932

