    scratch why work ["2 million+ dead, 1914–18","Not ready to fight","Hoped: limited aims"] x 822 y 210 font_size 22 hidden true

    card title "From peace to war, 1919–1939"

A: In twenty years Europe went from a peace treaty to a second world war. How?
    draw tl_base

B: The usual answer is Versailles: a peace so harsh that it made the next war.
    draw ev_vers

A: It was harsh: Germany lost thirteen per cent of its land and a tenth of its people.
    draw n_vers ver_box ver_line_1

A: One clause blamed it for the war, and it was billed 132 billion gold marks.
    draw bar_b bar_b_t

B: So Germany was crushed.
    point at.ref bar_b gesture underline

A: That's the myth. It paid about twenty billion before payments stopped in 1931.
    draw bar_p bar_p_t strike_b

    erase ver bar_b bar_b_t bar_p bar_p_t strike_b

A: And it was still the biggest power in the middle of Europe, among small new states. Angry, but not weak.
    draw smap s_t_de

    erase smap s_t_de

B: Then who was meant to keep the peace?
    draw n_league

A: The League of Nations. But the United States never joined, and the League had no army of its own.
    highlight n_league

A: In 1931 Japan seized Manchuria, and when the League objected, Japan simply left. In 1935 Mussolini's Italy invaded Abyssinia, and the sanctions spared oil.
    draw ev_manch ev_abyss

B: Then came the crash of 1929. By 1932 six million Germans were out of work.
    draw ev_crash n_dep

    draw vote

    ask "What share did they win in July 1932? Click on the line." on vote store g default "20"
        intro: In 1928 the Nazis had won just 2.6 per cent of the vote.
        right: Close: you said {g}; it was 37.3 per cent, the largest party in Germany.
        wrong: You said {g}; it was 37.3 per cent. In four years they became the largest party in Germany.

A: Desperate voters turned to the extremes, and in January 1933 Hitler became chancellor.
    draw ev_hitler

    draw n_aims

A: His aims were no secret: tear up Versailles, bring all Germans into one Reich, and win living space, [de:Lebensraum], in the east.
    blocking false

    highlight n_aims

    pause 3.5

    point at.ref n_vers gesture underline

B: So a resented treaty, a toothless League, a slump, and a man who wanted war. That made war certain.
    draw n_poss1 ar1 ar2 ar3 ar4

A: Possible, not certain. Every step from here was a gamble that someone could have stopped.
    highlight n_poss1

    clear

    card title "The gambles, 1935–1939"

A: Here is the middle of Europe in 1935: Germany, with Austria and Czechoslovakia to the south, and Poland to the east.
    draw m_uk t_uk m_fr t_fr m_de t_de m_ep t_ep m_at t_at m_cz t_cz m_pl t_pl m_su t_su parallel true

    draw d_de

A: March 1935: Hitler brought back conscription, openly breaking the treaty. There were protests, and nothing more.
    blocking false

    highlight d_de

    pause 3

    point at.ref m_de gesture circle

A: March 1936: German troops marched into the Rhineland, the strip the treaty kept empty of soldiers.
    draw m_rh t_rh

B: Couldn't France just march in?
    point at.ref m_fr gesture circle

A: France could have. Hitler later said those forty-eight hours were the most nerve-racking of his life. France did nothing.
    highlight m_rh

B: So why did Britain and France keep giving way?
    draw why_box

A: Memory, first: more than two million of their men had died in 1914 to 1918.
    draw why_line_1

A: Their generals said they were not ready, and many hoped his aims were limited: Germans rejoining Germany. That policy was appeasement.
    draw why_line_2 why_line_3

B: And did giving way work?
    erase why

A: Not for long. March 1938: German troops crossed into Austria, and it was annexed, the [de:Anschluss].
    draw m_at_taken d_at

A: September 1938, at Munich: Britain and France handed him the Sudetenland, the German-speaking rim of Czechoslovakia.
    draw m_sud t_sud

B: And Chamberlain flew home promising peace for our time.
    highlight m_sud

    draw m_czl d_cz

A: Six months later, in March 1939, Hitler took the rest of the Czech lands. These were Czechs, not Germans, and the hope of limited aims died.
    blocking false

    highlight m_czl

    pause 4

    point at.ref m_sud gesture circle

A: Britain now promised to defend Poland. Hitler's (@draw m_pact@) answer, in August: a pact with Stalin to carve Poland up between them.

A: On the first of September Germany invaded Poland. On the third, Britain and France declared war.
    draw m_warsaw t_war m_inv_w m_inv_n m_inv_s d_pl parallel true

B: Surely he'd expected that?
    point at.ref m_uk gesture circle

A: He had bet they wouldn't. A week before, he told his generals he had seen his enemies at Munich, and called them worms.
    highlight m_sud

    clear

    card title "Why war came in 1939"

    cards sort
        Made war possible: Versailles, Weak League, Depression
        Made it come in 1939: Rhineland ignored, Munich deal, Stalin pact

    ask "Sort each cause into its box." on sort store f after keep stage own
        intro: Some causes made war possible; others made it come in 1939.
        right: {f} right: the long causes set the stage, the unanswered steps set the date.
        wrong: {f} of {f.total} on the first try.

    erase sort

B: So did Hitler plan all this?
    draw tz_base ez_conscr ez_rhine ez_ansch ez_munich ez_prague ez_pact ez_poland parallel true

A: Historians still argue. A. J. P. Taylor called him an opportunist; most now say he had aims, and took each chance he was given.
    blocking false

    highlight ez_rhine

    pause 2.5

    highlight ez_munich

    pause 2.5

    highlight ez_prague

A: The long causes built the danger; what turned it into war was a lesson, taught step by step.
    draw long n_possible br1 br2 br3 br4 n_steps n_war ar_pw ar_sw

B: So every step that went unanswered told him the next one was safe.
    flow ar_sw

A: Until Poland, when it wasn't. And by then, stopping him meant a world war.
    draw box_war

    quiz "Suppose France had marched into the Rhineland in March 1936. On this argument, what was most likely?"
        right: He pulls back: his own generals expected to retreat, and a bluff that fails makes the next one look dangerous.
        wrong: Think about what each unanswered step taught him about the next one.
        * War comes anyway, on the same timetable
        + Hitler pulls back, and the next gamble looks far riskier
        * Nothing changes: the long causes decide

